      qtype: 'to-what-extent',
      prompt: 'To what extent does the play\'s ending fully resolve the power dynamic established between Prospero and Caliban in Act 1?',
      marks: 5,
      stimulusHtml: null,
      modelAnswerHtml: 'The play resolves its revenge plot against the nobles decisively — Prospero explicitly chooses "virtue" over "vengeance" and renounces his power. But its colonial power dynamic with Caliban is left far more ambiguous: Prospero\'s final line, "this thing of darkness, I acknowledge mine", is deliberately unclear about whether he is acknowledging Caliban as a responsibility, a possession, or an aspect of himself. The play therefore resolves one strand of its power theme (revenge against equals) while leaving another (colonial authority over a subordinate) conspicuously open.',
