      modelAnswerHtml: 'Hag-Seed keeps Shakespeare\'s underlying conclusion — that mercy ultimately outweighs vengeance — but arrives at it through a very different process. Where Prospero\'s renunciation is a single, morally absolute turn justified by Jacobean Christian providence, Felix\'s realisation that "his enemies had suffered, which had been a pleasure… then Felix had strewn forgiveness around, which had been a greater pleasure" is gradual, self-interested and explicitly psychological rather than moral. The conclusion is therefore not fully different (both privilege mercy) but its mechanism is: Atwood substitutes an observable psychological process for Shakespeare\'s theological one.',
